*Description of problem - Provide a detailed description of the issue encountered, including logs/command-output snippets and screenshots if the issue is observed in the UI:*
{code:java}
This is an RFE to have osd_mclock_skip_benchmark default to true. We keep seeing customers with osds benchmarking with super low iops resulting is huge decrease in performance.{code}
 

*The OCP platform infrastructure and deployment type (AWS, Bare Metal, VMware, etc. Please clarify if it is platform agnostic deployment), (IPI/UPI):*
{code:java}
All{code}
 

*The ODF deployment type (Internal, External, Internal-Attached (LSO), Multicluster, DR, Provider, etc):*
{code:java}
All
{code}
 

*The version of all relevant components (OCP, ODF, RHCS, ACM whichever is applicable):*
{code:java}
RHCS 6.X+{code}
 

*Is there any workaround available to the best of your knowledge?*

Yes, turn it off:
{code:java}
$ ceph config set global osd_mclock_skip_benchmark true   
$ ceph config set osd osd_mclock_override_recovery_settings true   
$ for X in $(ceph osd ls); do ceph config rm osd.${X} osd_mclock_max_capacity_iops_ssd; sleep 0.2; done   
$ for X in $(ceph osd ls); do ceph config rm osd.${X} osd_mclock_max_capacity_iops_hdd; sleep 0.2; done   
$ ceph config set osd osd_mclock_iops_capacity_threshold_ssd 98000.000000 
$ ceph config set osd osd_mclock_iops_capacity_threshold_ssd 98000.000000  
$ ceph config set osd osd_mclock_max_capacity_iops_ssd 98000.000000   {code}
Also outlined in the KCS [https://access.redhat.com/solutions/7092973]

 

*Can this issue be reproduced? If so, please provide the hit rate*
{code:java}
Depends on the env{code}

